            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the French for the school report, then the French for the head teacher."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: la faculté",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "la faculté — \"the faculty, the university; the ability\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Elle étudie à la faculté de médecine. She studies at the faculty of medicine."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "In speech, la fac: aller à la fac. It can also mean an ability."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "faculté is a learned borrowing from Latin facultās \"ability, power\" (Lewis & Short), from facilis \"easy, doable\"."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One more word for telling, explaining and describing at B1."
            }
          ]
        },
